# Search indexing: incremental by document, with a Rust destination

Scope: the xapian search index pipeline — today gn3's Python indexer
(scripts/index-genenetwork) — and how it becomes incremental.
Measurements are from two instrumented full runs on 2026-10-11
against the public 2025-02-18 database dump plus RDF caches
(47,040 documents; the instrumented build is byte-identical to the
reference index). Shares are what transfer: the absolute seconds are
single-host, warm-page-cache numbers, and the cache stage was a
warm-disk replay — a cold run pays a network SPARQL crawl instead.

## What exists today (verified in source and measurement)

- Every document already carries a stable unique identity term —
  constructed as `Q<doctype>:<name>:<dataset>`, lowercased — 47,040
  distinct, no duplicates, written through `replace_document`, so
  updates-by-key are supported by the format since the beginning.
- Change detection already exists, but only as an all-or-nothing
  external gate: `is_data_modified` compares stored per-table
  `CHECKSUM TABLE` values and a hash over the Virtuoso TTL directory
  (`md5hash_ttl_dir`, stored as `generif-checksum`), then answers
  one question — "did anything change?" — with an exit code. One
  changed row triggers the same full rebuild as a thousand.
- Where a full rebuild actually spends its time (two runs, 4.7%
  apart — measured on the public dump, whose gene side is truncated:
  100,000 ProbeSetXRef rows against millions in production, so the
  ~1.5% database-read share does NOT transfer; on the gene side the
  crawl is a real cost at production scale, which is why cheap
  row-count signals must short-circuit a run first):
  - ~47% replaying/reprocessing the SPARQL-derived wiki/rif text
    caches (independent of any database change)
  - ~30% xapian document writes
  - ~18% term generation and document build
  - ~1.5% database reads on the measured data

## The design

### Stage 0 — per-document granularity in the current indexer

Keep the existing gate as the outer trigger, and add granularity
underneath it:

- For each document, hash its complete source data: the full joined
  row set it is built from, the dataset label, and the cache text it
  consumed — keyed by its identity term. Because the dataset label
  is part of the input, mirrored datasets (two labels over the same
  rows) each carry their own key, and a shared-row change surfaces as
  a delta on both. Any change, from the database or the caches,
  surfaces as a source-hash delta on exactly the affected documents.
- A run crawls (cheap: ~1.5% of rebuild time), compares source
  hashes against the stored set, and rewrites only changed
  documents. Cache text is re-derived only for documents whose rows
  changed or whose consumed cache slices changed: slices are keyed
  by symbol, so each document records which slice identities it
  consumed, and a cache change is mapped to documents through the
  symbol→document relation. The ~47% replay applies only when cache
  content actually changes — not on every run.
- Documents whose source rows disappeared are deleted by identity
  term. The stored key set — not the index, not the crawl — is the
  source of truth for existence; a dataset withdrawn from
  configuration deletes all of its documents the same way.
- When nothing changed — the common case — the run writes nothing.
- Updates land in the live index: gn3 opens the index per request
  (search.py holds the database open only for the duration of one
  query), so a per-document update is visible to the next request
  with no service stop and no change to gn3 itself.
- Source hashes live in a sidecar file next to the index, not in
  index metadata (no new failure mode inside the index itself).
  Write order: index commit first, sidecar rename strictly after.
  A startup check compares the sidecar against the index's stored
  state; on mismatch, the next run falls back to a full
  crawl-and-compare.
- Stage 0 is timeboxed: it proves the semantics in production, and
  is replaced wholesale by stage 1, not maintained alongside it.

### Stage 1 — the Rust port

The indexer moves into gn4's Rust codebase, sharing one set of
schema definitions (document data, value slots, term scheme,
identity terms) with the native search engine — writer and reader
compile against the same types, so writer/reader drift becomes a
compile error instead of a bug report.

- Same semantics as stage 0, proven equal by the gates below.
- The from-scratch rebuild path stays as bootstrap and as the
  corruption-recovery tool.
- The Python indexer is retired when the Rust path has passed the
  gates on real data.

## Write path and sidecar (Rust port, design note 2026-10-13)

Measurements below are single-host, warm-page-cache, on the public
2025-02-18 dump and its RDF caches. Shares are expected to transfer;
absolute seconds do not. They describe the Rust write path and sidecar;
the from-scratch rebuild stays as bootstrap and recovery.

Finding that shaped the design: the per-document *build* cost is
dominated by term generation, not by the change hash. On the warm
incremental sweep, term generation and document assembly is ~94-95%
of the build phase; canonical encoding ~1.3%; the SHA-256 digest
~2.7-3.6%. The sidecar therefore keeps a cryptographic SHA-256
digest: replacing it would optimise a few percent and trade away
collision resistance.

Write path (measured, warm incremental): documents are built one at
a time and written as they are produced, rather than building the
whole document set in memory first. On a corpus scaled to ~10x
(458,452 documents), peak resident memory fell from ~12.0 GiB to
~1.6 GiB, and the full build time from ~1393 s to ~494 s; live
document objects are constant. The small-corpus build carries a few
seconds of streaming overhead. (The ~1.6 GiB figure is with the
source-hash set still monolithic.)

Sidecar (design, implementation in progress): sharded by dataset,
with a small manifest carrying the format version, document count,
and a content hash per shard. The manifest is written strictly after
the index commit and after the shards (each as temp + rename).
Startup verifies a shard's content hash before parsing it, and any
mismatch falls back to a full compare. An identity pre-pass checks
`name:dataset` uniqueness before any write (part of the measured write
path above), and shard-scoped processing targets resident memory
bounded by the largest dataset rather than the whole corpus; that
bound is not yet measured.

```
        crawl (rows grouped by dataset)
        |
        v
        identity pre-pass (uniqueness before any write)
        |
        v
        build one doc  (no all-docs fan-in)
        |
        v
        replace_document (by idterm)
        |
        v
        commit every N docs
        |
        v
        sidecar.d/<dataset>.json shards (temp + rename)
        |
        v
        manifest.json (version, doc_count, per-shard sha256)   [written last]
```

## The gates

1. Idempotence: an incremental run after no changes writes nothing;
   the index is untouched.
2. Correctness: after simulated changes (add, update, and delete),
   the incrementally-updated index and a from-scratch rebuild of
   the same data are equal document-for-document — same identity
   terms, document data, termlists, value slots, and positions.
   (After real incremental updates the on-disk files legitimately
   differ from a rebuild — docid assignment and chunk layout are
   not deterministic — so equality is defined at document level,
   not byte level.) Deletion is part of this gate explicitly: an
   incremental path that cannot remove documents is not
   incremental.
3. Twin-index equivalence for the Rust port: the Rust indexer's
   output is compared document-for-document (identity, data,
   termlist, value slots, positions) against the Python indexer's
   output on the same data — including the known quirks the
   comparison must preserve verbatim: active English stemming, the
   non-functional stopper configuration, and the occupied slots
   0–5.

## Open questions

- The trigger and schedule for runs (cron interval, daemon, or
  on-event) — the design is trigger-agnostic; the choice is
  operational.
- Whether production enables row-based binlog, which would later
  allow exact row-level change feeds as an alternative to the
  crawl-and-compare approach.
- The measured change rate of the production database — the
  crawl-and-compare design does not depend on it, but it informs
  the schedule choice.

